<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">My&nbsp;take&nbsp;on&nbsp;it</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Its&nbsp;all&nbsp;about&nbsp;the&nbsp;balancing&nbsp;act&nbsp;with&nbsp;this&nbsp;sort&nbsp;of&nbsp;article</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>On 04/12/2011 at approximately 3:15 AM, SCP-2590 travelled to the location of an abandoned warehouse&#8230;<br />
[&#8230;]<br />
&#8230;investigation. The following is a log of the first communications with Alpha Squad Leader:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So far, so good. This is well trod territory for the Foundation, but has the appropriate sort of &quot;just another day at the foundation&quot; vibe to go with it. Some tightening wouldn't hurt- &quot;SCP-2590 was found {idling/parked/doing donuts?}&quot;, for example- and you might break up the information and order of events a little.<br />
Start with an initial report log</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Alpha Squad Leader:</strong> &quot;SCP-2590 is slowly driving toward what looks like a descent to an underground tunnel, straight ahead from the entrance to the warehouse&#8230;&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>and have them instructed to follow. Think swat team or special forces- they get trained in standard procedures to follow for scenarios like &quot;an anomaly enters an area of suspected atypical space-time&quot;, which would probably include some sort of &quot;should we pursue?&quot; option, since some of these things are one way. The &quot;have two stay behind and the rest follow&quot; does seem like a reasonable way to go from there, however.</p>
<p>That would also let you focus the &quot;re-establishing contact&quot; log on what happened after they lost contact, rather than having to backtrack with information that the foundation should have already known (what could they see upon arrival at the warehouse).</p>
<p>The last two things I'd suggest ditching entirely (&quot;+ List of SCP-2590 objects, post-Incident 2590-3&quot; and the paragraph that starts &quot;SCP-2590 came to the Foundation's notice when&#8230;&quot;)</p>
<p>There's only one in the post incident list that didn't tip over into creepypasta territory for me:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>#6 Written in [DATA EXPUNGED] on parchment. I'm just delivering the message.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The others come across as rather campy, but have that described as written in black on a white sheet of paper after it tried to get them to follow it to wherever it went&#8230; that's an ending there.</p>
</div>
</div>
